WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
When you're designing AI systems, one of the first questions you'll hit is this,

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.600
what actually makes something agentic? In this lesson, we'll compare agents,

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.719
workflows, and chatbots, figure out why an LLM by itself isn't an agent, and explore how tool

4
00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:23.280
use closes that gap. By the end of this lesson, you'll know how to tell these architectures apart

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.680
and when each one makes sense in your work. The word agentic gets attached to almost

6
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.520
anything with a language model inside it, so let's draw the lines precisely.

7
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.720
An agentic system is one where the language model autonomously pursues a goal.

8
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.360
It perceives context, selects actions, invokes tools, and iterates until it's done. A deterministic

9
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.880
workflow is different. The developer writes the control flow at design time, and the model just

10
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.119
fills in content along the way. An autonomous agent goes further still. It operates without

11
00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:02.400
step-by-step human direction, making its own planning decisions to hit a high-level goal.

12
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.440
Autonomy exists on a spectrum, from fully supervised to fully autonomous,

13
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.319
and most real systems sit somewhere in the middle.

14
00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:17.040
A conversational chatbot looks powerful, but its architecture is actually quite narrow.

15
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.559
It receives your message and generates a reply. That's the complete loop. There's no mechanism for

16
00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:28.239
the chatbot to open a file, call an API, or remember anything outside the current context

17
00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:34.480
window. This isn't a criticism. Chatbots are the right tool for a huge range of conversational

18
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.319
tasks, but they are fundamentally stateless text generators. The moment you want the system to take

19
00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:45.599
an action in the world, you've left chatbot territory. You need tool use, and you need

20
00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:52.000
something deciding when and how to use those tools. The comparison that trips people up most

21
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.319
is workflow versus agent. In a deterministic workflow, a developer writes every step, every

22
00:01:58.319 --> 00:02:05.199
branch, every loop before the system runs. The LLM might be involved at several points, generating

23
00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:11.759
summaries or filling in structured outputs, but it never decides what happens next. That's fixed.

24
00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:18.320
An agentic system flips this. The control flow emerges at inference time. The model looks at

25
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.320
its current context, decides which tool to call, observes the result, and then decides what to do

26
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:30.800
next. That runtime decision making is the defining characteristic. It's also what makes agents harder

27
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.600
to audit and higher risk. Here's a question worth sitting with. If LLMs are so capable,

28
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.759
why aren't they agents by default? The answer is that a language model's only output is tokens.

29
00:02:43.759 --> 00:02:50.479
It generates text. It has no native ability to read a file, execute code, query a database,

30
00:02:50.479 --> 00:02:55.199
or observe anything that happens outside the current inference call. There's no persistent

31
00:02:55.199 --> 00:03:01.600
memory, no side effects, no feedback loop. We call this the action environment gap. The model can

32
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.039
describe what it would do, but it can't actually do it. Closing that gap requires a runtime layer

33
00:03:07.039 --> 00:03:12.559
that takes the model's structured output, executes the requested action, and feeds the result back

34
00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:19.759
into context as an observation. So how do we actually close that gap? The answer is a building

35
00:03:19.759 --> 00:03:26.080
block Anthropic calls the augmented LLM. Take a base language model and enhance it with retrieval,

36
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.520
tools, and memory. That combination is the foundation every agentic system is built from.

37
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.440
What makes it powerful is that the model uses these capabilities actively. It generates its

38
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.479
own search queries when it needs information. It selects the right tool for the job,

39
00:03:42.479 --> 00:03:48.479
and it decides what's worth remembering across steps. The model isn't a passive text generator

40
00:03:48.479 --> 00:03:54.559
anymore. It's directing its own access to information and actions. Once you see the augmented

41
00:03:54.559 --> 00:04:00.559
LLM as the base unit, everything else in agent design is about how you wire these units together

42
00:04:00.559 --> 00:04:06.320
and control what they're allowed to do. We can break the agentic loop into four pillars.

43
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.720
Perception. The model reads its context, which includes messages, injected data, and results

44
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.720
from previous tool calls. Selection. It decides which action or tool to invoke given the current

45
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:26.000
goal state. Execution. The runtime calls that tool and captures whatever it returns. And iteration.

46
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.440
The result is fed back into context as an observation, and the model plans its next step.

47
00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:36.959
This loop continues until a termination condition is met. Either the goal is achieved,

48
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:43.279
a limit is hit, or an error requires escalation. Understanding this loop is fundamental to

49
00:04:43.279 --> 00:04:50.160
designing, debugging, and auditing agentic systems. Claude's tool system is the practical

50
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.839
implementation of everything we just described. You define tools using a schema, a name,

51
00:04:55.839 --> 00:05:02.239
a description in natural language, and a parameter specification. When Claude is generating a response

52
00:05:02.239 --> 00:05:08.480
and determines a tool is needed, it emits a structured tool call output rather than plain

53
00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.799
text. The runtime intercepts that, executes the actual function or API call, and returns the

54
00:05:14.799 --> 00:05:21.040
result to Claude as an observation in the next context window. One thing that surprises

55
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.720
practitioners, the description field is the most important part of the schema. Claude selects tools

56
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:33.600
based on how well the description matches the task. A vague description means unreliable tool

57
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.000
selection. So when should you actually build an agentic system? Less often than you'd think.

58
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.920
If the steps to complete a task can be written out in advance, a deterministic workflow is usually

59
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.160
the better choice. It's predictable, auditable, and easier to test. Prompt chaining is a good

60
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.640
example. Each model call feeds the next, which looks agentic from the outside, but the developer

61
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:05.679
fixed that sequence at design time. Agents shine when the path to the goal can't be prescripted,

62
00:06:05.679 --> 00:06:11.119
when the system needs to adapt based on intermediate results. But higher autonomy

63
00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:17.119
means higher risk. Actions have real-world consequences, and mistakes can propagate

64
00:06:17.119 --> 00:06:23.279
across several steps before anyone notices. Minimal footprint and human in the loop checkpoints are the

65
00:06:23.279 --> 00:06:30.079
standard answer to that. The word agentic gets stretched to cover anything with a model inside

66
00:06:30.079 --> 00:06:36.720
it. What actually separates the categories is who decides the next step and when. A developer

67
00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.320
fixing the sequence at design time gives you a workflow. A model choosing at inference time

68
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.600
gives you an agent. That's why one is easy to audit and the other is challenging.

69
00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.440
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

